    "result_text": "Green. Both findings addressed.\n\n## What I changed\n\n**Finding 1 (stale cross-reference) \u2014 fixed.** `OwnerPostcode`'s `CITY_REGION` comment pointed at `Owner.getLocality()` for its exact city\u2192region behaviour, which no longer lives there after the redesign (`getLocality()` now just parses the customerCode prefix). Rather than repoint a comment on a table that was itself redundant, I removed the table and repointed the class javadoc at the new authoritative methods `Owner.regionForCity` / `Owner.regionForPostcode`.\n\n**Finding 2 (missed consolidation) \u2014 fixed.** `OwnerPostcode` carried hand-maintained private copies of `CITY_REGION` and `REGION_RANGE` identical to `Owner`'s. I made `Owner` the single authority by decomposing its region rule into two public primitives:\n- `regionForCity(city)` \u2014 exact city\u2192region, `null` for unknown cities.\n- `regionForPostcode(postcode)` \u2014 region whose range contains the postcode, `null` otherwise.\n\n`regionFor(postcode, city)` is now built on those two, and `OwnerPostcode.isValid` delegates to them, so the region tables exist in exactly one place.\n\n**Behaviour preserved.** `isValid` keeps the same semantics: absent/blank postcode \u2192 valid; non-4-digit \u2192 invalid; unknown city \u2192 any 4-digit accepted; known city \u2192 postcode must resolve to that same region. Since regions have distinct non-overlapping ranges, `region.equals(Owner.regionForPostcode(postcode))` is equivalent to the old \"value within the city-region's range\" check. The only divergence is the old `if (range == null) return true` branch, which was unreachable dead code \u2014 every city in the table maps to a region that has a range.\n\nTest suite: **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.",
